{
  "id": "05-trapping-rain-water",
  "title": "Trapping Rain Water",
  "track": "Two Pointers",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "topics": [
    "two-pointers",
    "arrays"
  ],
  "source": "canonical Trapping Rain Water concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The input is an array of non-negative integers height, where height[i] is the elevation of the bar at position i and every bar is 1 unit wide. When rain falls, water collects over each position up to the lower of the tallest bar on its left and the tallest on its right. Return the total units of trapped water.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(height) <= 5000",
    "0 <= height[i] <= 1000"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    "height"
  ],
  "functionName": "trap",
  "starterCode": "def trap(height):\n    # Return the total units of water trapped over the bars.\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": {
        "height": [
          0,
          2,
          0,
          3,
          1,
          0,
          1,
          3,
          2,
          1
        ]
      },
      "output": 9,
      "explanation": "Water sits over positions 2, 4, 5, and 6: 2 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 9 units."
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "height": [
          4,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      "output": 1,
      "explanation": "The middle bar holds min(4, 3) - 2 = 1 unit."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The water over one position depends only on the tallest bar to its left, the tallest to its right, and the bar itself.",
    "Water[i] = max(0, min(maxLeft(i), maxRight(i)) - height[i]). Computing both maxima fresh for every position repeats the same scans.",
    "One pass builds maxLeft prefix values, another builds maxRight suffix values; a third pass sums the water. A two-pointer variant reaches the same in one pass with O(1) space."
  ],
  "solution": "The water over position i is min(tallest bar left of i, tallest bar right of i) minus height[i], when positive. Prefix and suffix maxima make each lookup constant: three passes, O(n) time, O(n) space. The two-pointer technique compresses this to one pass and O(1) space by moving the side with the smaller known maximum.",
  "solutionCode": "def trap(height):\n    left, right = 0, len(height) - 1\n    left_max = right_max = 0\n    total = 0\n    while left < right:\n        if height[left] < height[right]:\n            if height[left] >= left_max:\n                left_max = height[left]\n            else:\n                total += left_max - height[left]\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            if height[right] >= right_max:\n                right_max = height[right]\n            else:\n                total += right_max - height[right]\n            right -= 1\n    return total\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def trap(height):\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(len(height)):\n        left = 0\n        for j in range(i):\n            if height[j] > left:\n                left = height[j]\n        right = 0\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(height)):\n            if height[j] > right:\n                right = height[j]\n        level = left if left < right else right\n        if level > height[i]:\n            total += level - height[i]\n    return total\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include flat arrays, single bars, monotonic slopes, and tall walls around low ground",
  "expectedComplexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
